  <h2>What BSA Measures</h2>
  <p>Body Surface Area (BSA) is the total area of the exterior surface of the human body, expressed in square meters. For an average adult, BSA is approximately 1.9 m² — about the size of a small bathroom floor. Children have smaller BSAs (a 10-year-old is around 1.1 m²), and neonates are around 0.25 m².</p>
  <p>BSA correlates better than body weight with metabolic mass — the part of the body that interacts with drugs, performs work, and consumes oxygen. This is why clinical pharmacology has used BSA as a dosing reference for nearly a century.</p>

  <h2>The Mosteller Formula (1987)</h2>
  <p>The most widely used BSA formula today is the Mosteller equation, published by RD Mosteller in the <em>New England Journal of Medicine</em> in 1987:</p>
  <p><code>BSA (m²) = √(height_cm × weight_kg ÷ 3600)</code></p>
  <p>For an adult at 175 cm and 70 kg:</p>
  <p><code>BSA = √(175 × 70 ÷ 3600) = √3.403 = 1.84 m²</code></p>
  <p>Mosteller's formula replaced the earlier DuBois formula (1916) for many clinical uses because the arithmetic is far simpler and accuracy is comparable. Both remain in use; Mosteller is more common in modern oncology and critical care.</p>

  <h2>Clinical Applications</h2>
  <p>BSA is the standard metric in several clinical contexts:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Chemotherapy dosing:</strong> Most cytotoxic drugs are dosed in mg per m². This narrows the variability in drug exposure across patients of different body sizes compared with flat dosing.</li>
    <li><strong>Cardiac index:</strong> Cardiac output (L/min) divided by BSA gives cardiac index (L/min/m²), which is more comparable across patients than raw cardiac output.</li>
    <li><strong>Burn assessment:</strong> The percentage of body surface area burned (using the Rule of Nines) determines fluid resuscitation protocols and prognosis.</li>
    <li><strong>Pediatric drug dosing:</strong> Many pediatric medications are dosed per m² rather than per kg because of the better correlation with metabolic mass.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Reference Values</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Population</th><th>Typical BSA</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Adult men (average)</td><td>~2.0 m²</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Adult women (average)</td><td>~1.7 m²</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Children (10 years)</td><td>~1.1 m²</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Neonates</td><td>~0.25 m²</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Obese adults</td><td>2.5–3.0 m²</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Limitations of BSA</h2>
  <p>BSA is a geometric construct — it does not directly measure metabolic mass, body composition, or organ function. For obesity pharmacology in particular, BSA-based dosing is increasingly questioned because adipose tissue is less metabolically active than lean mass. Drugs dosed per m² may be overdosed in patients with very high BMI.</p>
  <p>For most clinical purposes, however, Mosteller's formula remains the standard. Its simplicity and reproducibility make it preferable to alternatives, and its limitations are well understood.</p>

